  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "In a severe car crash, an airbag works with your seat belt to cushion you from hard interior surfaces. This explanation slows the sequence down. In a real crash, the airbag responds in tens of milliseconds. Crash sensors detect the vehicle's sudden deceleration and send that information to the airbag control unit. The control unit triggers the airbag only when the crash pattern indicates a severe impact. A small igniter then activates a gas generator inside the folded airbag module. The generator rapidly produces gas that flows into the woven fabric cushion. The expanding airbag spreads out in front of the occupant, distributing force over a wider area. As you press into the cushion, vents let gas escape, so the airbag can absorb motion while deflating. So the airbag protects by sensing a crash, inflating quickly and then yielding in a controlled way.",
